{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent returns are negative.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model extrapolates the prior uptrend and reacts too slowly to regime shifts; after a peak it keeps predicting elevated/renewed highs while the market sells off. Add a regime-switch trigger: if the last 5–10 days show a clear lower-high/lower-low pattern, force the forecast drift negative.",
    "Insufficient mean reversion: forecasts revert upward toward the recent 30d average even when the true process mean-reverts downward to a new lower level. Use a stronger mean-reversion anchor to the most recent 5–10 day mean (not the 30d mean) during selloffs.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: the model predicts sizable rebounds (and even new highs) after initial declines; realized rebounds are smaller and fade. Cap rebound amplitude as a fraction of the preceding drawdown (e.g., rebound ≤ 30–40% of the last 10d peak-to-trough move).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: overprediction increases with forecast horizon (later days are most biased high). Apply a horizon-increasing downward adjustment (e.g., subtract an offset that grows with lead time) when the short-term slope is negative.",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast paths are too smooth and centered too high; realized paths show sustained step-downs. When a break occurs (large negative day + follow-through), shift the entire remaining trajectory down rather than gradually drifting."
  ]
}
